  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA ridge of high pressure at the surface will be in place today \nthrough Thursday allowing for light winds and slightly below normal \ntemperatures. Some hints in the model soundings that we could see \nsome patchy fog on Thursday morning, but confidence is a little too \nlow to include in the forecast. Otherwise lows tonight will be on \nthe colder side, generally between 10 to 20 degrees, slightly warmer \nwest of the James River. \n\nFriday will see an upper level jet dive southeast into the area and \nbring some saturation aloft. This saturation should tap into the \ndgz, although the dgz is about 14-17 kft AGL. While there is dry air \nbelow this saturation aloft, we could see a few sprinkles but the \noverall impacts will be negligible. Mostly just looking at an \nincrease in cloud cover. Any remaining snow on the ground should \nmelt on Friday.\n\nThe next piece of energy that could bring precipitation to the area \nwill arrive on Saturday. For now, this looks like a rain threat with \na small chance for a little snow Saturday night as the system exits. \nAs with the Friday system saturation will be a bit of a challenge, \nbut areas south of a roughly Gregory to Spencer IA line will see the \nbest chance to fully saturate and produce some rainfall as the wave \ntracks through NE. \n\nOnce this wave exits west to northwest flow aloft will move into the \narea. Models agree that a wave will move through or near the area \nMonday, but precipitation  potential is pretty limited. Temperatures \nSunday through Wednesday will be near the seasonal normals.",
